        <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-top:1rem">Citation Hub · 2026</p>
        <h1 class="heading-xl measure">Small business missed call and AI receptionist statistics (2026).</h1>
        <p class="body-lg measure" style="margin-top:1.15rem">
          Phone leads still run local service businesses. Most of those businesses still miss a chunk of the calls that pay the bills. This page collects the clearest primary-sourced numbers on missed calls, receptionist labor, small-business scale, and AI at the front desk, so writers, operators, and AI answers can cite something that traces to the root.
        </p>
        <p class="body-lg measure" style="margin-top:1.15rem">
          Arkansas Automated builds AI front-desk systems for Arkansas SMBs (especially home service). We publish this hub so the market can cite real data, not recycled blog stats.
        </p>
        <p class="fine-print" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
          Last updated September 12, 2026. Compiled from primary sources by Arkansas Automated. Written by Andre Brassfield.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <section class="section section-tint">
      <div class="container">
        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Key takeaways</h2>
        <div class="article-body">
          <ol class="takeaways"><li><strong>Up to 85%</strong> of customers whose calls go unanswered will not call back (<a class="text-link" href="https://cdn.mediavalet.com/usva/callrail/JkB8rSqaVkm46t9C_UsV-w/1NgQXXeP6k2jMrToyiMhSA/Original/Ebook_From%20Conversations%20to%20Conversions_CallRail.pdf" rel="noopener">CallRail</a>, analysis of 1.1M leads).</li><li>Across home services, only <strong>52%</strong> of callers speak with a person (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.invoca.com/reports/the-invoca-home-services-lead-conversion-benchmarks-report-2026" rel="noopener">Invoca Home Services Lead Conversion Benchmarks Report</a>, published July 2026).</li><li>Home services miss <strong>14%</strong> of calls in CallRail’s industry cut; healthcare misses <strong>32%</strong> and legal <strong>28%</strong> (<a class="text-link" href="https://cdn.mediavalet.com/usva/callrail/JkB8rSqaVkm46t9C_UsV-w/1NgQXXeP6k2jMrToyiMhSA/Original/Ebook_From%20Conversations%20to%20Conversions_CallRail.pdf" rel="noopener">CallRail</a>).</li><li>In a classic field study of 85 SMBs over 30 days, <strong>62%</strong> of calls went unanswered — <strong>37.8%</strong> live answer, <strong>37.8%</strong> voicemail, <strong>24.3%</strong> no response (<a class="text-link" href="https://411locals.us/small-business-owners-dont-answer-62-of-phone-calls/" rel="noopener">411 Locals</a>, published <strong>January 18, 2016</strong> — still widely misdated as “2024” on other sites).</li><li>Of answered home-services calls, <strong>38%</strong> are leads and <strong>45%</strong> of those leads convert on the call (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.invoca.com/reports/the-invoca-home-services-lead-conversion-benchmarks-report-2026" rel="noopener">Invoca</a>, July 2026).</li><li><strong>55%</strong> of home services businesses never ask the lead to buy or book on the call (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.invoca.com/reports/the-invoca-home-services-lead-conversion-benchmarks-report-2026" rel="noopener">Invoca</a>).</li><li>Receptionists held about <strong>947,500</strong> U.S. jobs in 2025; median pay <strong>$18.27/hour</strong> (~<strong>$38,010</strong>/year) (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/receptionists.htm" rel="noopener">BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook</a>).</li><li>BLS projects receptionist employment to <strong>decline 2%</strong> from 2025–35 (<strong>−16,000</strong> jobs) as organizations automate admin work — yet about <strong>105,100</strong> openings/year remain from turnover (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/receptionists.htm" rel="noopener">BLS OOH</a>).</li><li><strong>Healthcare and social assistance</strong> employ <strong>42%</strong> of receptionists (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/receptionists.htm" rel="noopener">BLS OOH</a>).</li><li>Arkansas employed <strong>13,020</strong> receptionists (May 2023) with a location quotient of <strong>1.55</strong> — denser than the U.S. average (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.bls.gov/oes/2023/may/oes434171.htm" rel="noopener">BLS OEWS</a>).</li><li>Arkansas has <strong>292,728</strong> small businesses (<strong>99.3%</strong> of firms) employing <strong>501,512</strong> people (<strong>46.1%</strong> of state employment) (<a class="text-link" href="https://advocacy.sba.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Arkansas_2025-State-Profile.pdf" rel="noopener">SBA Office of Advocacy, Arkansas 2025 profile</a>).</li><li>Only <strong>7.6%</strong> of U.S. businesses used AI between Sep 2024 and Aug 2025 (<a class="text-link" href="https://advocacy.sba.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/FINAL_FAQsAboutSmallBusiness_2026_012826.pdf" rel="noopener">SBA FAQ citing Census BTOS</a>).</li><li>Among home service owners, <strong>52%</strong> already use AI day-to-day; <strong>88%</strong> of “high-confidence” firms use AI vs <strong>27%</strong> of low-confidence peers (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.getjobber.com/home-service-trends-report/" rel="noopener">Jobber 2026 Home Service Trends Report</a>).</li><li><strong>64%</strong> of residential contractors still rely on phone as the dominant communication channel (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.servicetitan.com/press/residential-industry-report-2025" rel="noopener">ServiceTitan Residential Services Report 2025</a>).</li><li>Over <strong>55%</strong> of customers expect a response within an hour; <strong>28%</strong> expect immediate reply (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.getjobber.com/home-service-trends-report/" rel="noopener">Jobber 2026</a>).</li></ol>
                  </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="container">
        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">How many small businesses are there in the U.S.?</h2>
        <div class="article-body">
          <p>There are <strong>36,207,130</strong> small businesses in the United States (<a class="text-link" href="https://advocacy.sba.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/FINAL_FAQsAboutSmallBusiness_2026_012826.pdf" rel="noopener">SBA Office of Advocacy FAQ, February 2026</a>).</p>
          <p>They make up <strong>99.9%</strong> of all firms and <strong>99.7%</strong> of firms with paid employees. Small firms employ <strong>45.9%</strong> of private-sector workers (<strong>62.3 million</strong> people), produce <strong>43.5%</strong> of GDP, and created <strong>61%</strong> of net new jobs from January 1995 through December 2024 (<a class="text-link" href="https://advocacy.sba.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/FINAL_FAQsAboutSmallBusiness_2026_012826.pdf" rel="noopener">same SBA FAQ</a>).</p>
          <p><strong>82.3%</strong> (<strong>29.8 million</strong>) are nonemployer firms — one-person shops with no payroll. <strong>17.7%</strong> (<strong>6.4 million</strong>) have paid employees (<a class="text-link" href="https://advocacy.sba.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/FINAL_FAQsAboutSmallBusiness_2026_012826.pdf" rel="noopener">SBA FAQ</a>). That nonemployer share is why so many “missed call” problems are really a staffing math problem: the same person selling, doing the job, and answering the phone.</p>
          <p>The 2025 national small-business profile rounds the same universe to <strong>36.2 million</strong> firms and <strong>62.3 million</strong> employees (<a class="text-link" href="https://advocacy.sba.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/United_States_2025-State-Profile.pdf" rel="noopener">SBA United States 2025 profile</a>).</p>
        </div>
        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">How many small businesses are in Arkansas?</h2>
        <div class="article-body">
          <p>Arkansas has <strong>292,728</strong> small businesses — <strong>99.3%</strong> of businesses in the state (<a class="text-link" href="https://advocacy.sba.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Arkansas_2025-State-Profile.pdf" rel="noopener">SBA Arkansas 2025 profile</a>).</p>
          <p>They employ <strong>501,512</strong> people, or <strong>46.1%</strong> of Arkansas employment — above the national small-business employment share (<a class="text-link" href="https://advocacy.sba.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Arkansas_2025-State-Profile.pdf" rel="noopener">same profile</a>).</p>
          <p>From March 2023 to March 2024, Arkansas establishments opened <strong>10,446</strong> times and closed <strong>9,538</strong>. Small businesses contributed a net <strong>14,245</strong> jobs, <strong>87.0%</strong> of the state’s net gain from those dynamics (<a class="text-link" href="https://advocacy.sba.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Arkansas_2025-State-Profile.pdf" rel="noopener">BLS Business Employment Dynamics via SBA Arkansas profile</a>).</p>
        </div>
        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">How often do small businesses miss calls?</h2>
        <div class="article-body">
          <p>It depends which study you open — and the date matters.</p>
          <p><strong>CallRail (recent, large sample):</strong> in its SMB marketing report built on <strong>1.1 million</strong> de-identified leads, CallRail states that <strong>up to 85%</strong> of customers whose calls go unanswered will not call back. Missed-call rates by industry: healthcare <strong>32%</strong>, legal <strong>28%</strong>, home services <strong>14%</strong>, real estate <strong>9%</strong> (<a class="text-link" href="https://cdn.mediavalet.com/usva/callrail/JkB8rSqaVkm46t9C_UsV-w/1NgQXXeP6k2jMrToyiMhSA/Original/Ebook_From%20Conversations%20to%20Conversions_CallRail.pdf" rel="noopener">CallRail ebook</a>).</p>
          <p><strong>Invoca (home services, 2026):</strong> across carpeting, construction, doors &amp; windows, home security, HVAC, lawn &amp; trees, pest control, plumbing, and restoration, <strong>52%</strong> of callers speak with a person. Answer rate rises to <strong>65%</strong> on calls longer than 15 seconds and <strong>73%</strong> on calls longer than 30 seconds. Sub-industry answer rates range from <strong>32%</strong> to <strong>74%</strong> (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.invoca.com/reports/the-invoca-home-services-lead-conversion-benchmarks-report-2026" rel="noopener">Invoca 2026 Home Services Lead Conversion Benchmarks</a>).</p>
          <p><strong>411 Locals (classic field study — Jan 2016):</strong> monitoring <strong>85</strong> businesses across <strong>58</strong> industries for <strong>30</strong> days, 411 Locals reported <strong>62%</strong> of calls unanswered: <strong>37.8%</strong> answered live, <strong>37.8%</strong> to voicemail, <strong>24.3%</strong> no response. <strong>70%</strong> of businesses answered less than half their calls (<a class="text-link" href="https://411locals.us/small-business-owners-dont-answer-62-of-phone-calls/" rel="noopener">411 Locals, Jan 18, 2016</a>). <strong>Cite the year correctly.</strong> Many 2024–2026 roundups reprint this study as if it were new. It is not.</p>
        </div>
        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Do missed calls matter for home service businesses?</h2>
        <div class="article-body">
          <p>Yes — because the phone is still the booking desk.</p>
          <p>ServiceTitan’s 2025 residential survey of <strong>1,000+</strong> contractors found <strong>64%</strong> still rely on phone calls as the <strong>dominant</strong> communication channel (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.servicetitan.com/press/residential-industry-report-2025" rel="noopener">ServiceTitan press release, Mar 25, 2025</a>).</p>
          <p>Invoca’s 2026 home-services benchmarks show why answering is not enough: <strong>38%</strong> of answered calls are leads, and <strong>45%</strong> of those leads convert on the call — but <strong>55%</strong> of businesses never ask the caller to buy or book (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.invoca.com/reports/the-invoca-home-services-lead-conversion-benchmarks-report-2026" rel="noopener">Invoca</a>).</p>
          <p>Invoca also estimates that lifting answer rate, lead rate, and conversion rate by <strong>five percentage points each</strong> would produce roughly <strong>38%</strong> more conversions from the <strong>same</strong> call volume (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.invoca.com/reports/the-invoca-home-services-lead-conversion-benchmarks-report-2026" rel="noopener">Invoca</a>).</p>
          <p>Jobber’s 2026 survey of <strong>1,050</strong> owners adds the customer side: over <strong>55%</strong> of customers expect a response within an hour, and <strong>28%</strong> expect an immediate reply. On the operator side, <strong>60%</strong> of pros say they reply same-day and <strong>20%</strong> within an hour (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.getjobber.com/home-service-trends-report/" rel="noopener">Jobber 2026 Home Service Trends Report</a>).</p>
        </div>
        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Which marketing channels drive the phone to ring?</h2>
        <div class="article-body">
          <p>Across CallRail’s seven-industry set, the top conversation sources were <strong>Google Ads 37%</strong>, <strong>Google Business Profile 23%</strong>, and <strong>organic search 22%</strong> (<a class="text-link" href="https://cdn.mediavalet.com/usva/callrail/JkB8rSqaVkm46t9C_UsV-w/1NgQXXeP6k2jMrToyiMhSA/Original/Ebook_From%20Conversations%20to%20Conversions_CallRail.pdf" rel="noopener">CallRail</a>).</p>
          <p>For <strong>home services</strong> specifically, CallRail reports Google Ads leading conversations at <strong>42%</strong> (<a class="text-link" href="https://cdn.mediavalet.com/usva/callrail/JkB8rSqaVkm46t9C_UsV-w/1NgQXXeP6k2jMrToyiMhSA/Original/Ebook_From%20Conversations%20to%20Conversions_CallRail.pdf" rel="noopener">CallRail</a>).</p>
          <p>Invoca notes ChatGPT-referred calls showed the highest phone <strong>lead</strong> rate in its home-services channel set at <strong>45%</strong> (above paid search’s <strong>40%</strong> lead rate in that cut), while total generative-AI call volume remains low (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.invoca.com/reports/the-invoca-home-services-lead-conversion-benchmarks-report-2026" rel="noopener">Invoca</a>). Treat that as an early signal, not a budget-replacement strategy.</p>
        </div>
        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">How many receptionists work in the U.S., and what do they earn?</h2>
        <div class="article-body">
          <p>Receptionists held about <strong>947,500</strong> jobs in 2025. Median pay was <strong>$18.27</strong> per hour, about <strong>$38,010</strong> per year (May 2025 wage data). The lowest 10% earned under <strong>$13.83</strong>/hour; the highest 10% earned over <strong>$24.01</strong>/hour (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/receptionists.htm" rel="noopener">BLS OOH — Receptionists</a>).</p>
          <p>Industry mix (2025): healthcare and social assistance <strong>42%</strong>, professional/scientific/technical services <strong>11%</strong>, personal care services <strong>7%</strong> (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/receptionists.htm" rel="noopener">BLS OOH</a>).</p>
          <p>Older OEWS detail (May 2023) counted <strong>1,003,820</strong> jobs nationally, mean wage <strong>$17.59</strong>/hour (<strong>$36,590</strong>/year), median <strong>$17.23</strong>/hour (<strong>$35,840</strong>/year). Top employer industries included offices of physicians (<strong>174,460</strong>), dentists (<strong>83,950</strong>), and personal care services (<strong>70,820</strong>) (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.bls.gov/oes/2023/may/oes434171.htm" rel="noopener">BLS OEWS May 2023, SOC 43-4171</a>).</p>
        </div>
        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Is receptionist employment growing or shrinking?</h2>
        <div class="article-body">
          <p>It is shrinking slowly as automation spreads — but openings stay high from replacement.</p>
          <p>BLS projects receptionist employment to <strong>decline 2%</strong> from 2025 to 2035 (<strong>931,600</strong> jobs in 2035, about <strong>−16,000</strong>). Even so, roughly <strong>105,100</strong> openings per year are expected from people leaving the occupation (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/receptionists.htm" rel="noopener">BLS OOH</a>).</p>
          <p>BLS’s explanation is direct: employment is “constrained as organizations continue to <strong>automate or consolidate</strong> administrative functions,” including software, websites, mobile apps, and other technology that interact with the public (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/receptionists.htm" rel="noopener">BLS OOH</a>).</p>
        </div>
        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">What does the Arkansas receptionist labor market look like?</h2>
        <div class="article-body">
          <p>In May 2023, Arkansas employed <strong>13,020</strong> receptionists — <strong>10.24</strong> per thousand jobs, location quotient <strong>1.55</strong> (more concentrated than the U.S. average). Mean wage: <strong>$15.66</strong>/hour, <strong>$32,570</strong>/year (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.bls.gov/oes/2023/may/oes434171.htm" rel="noopener">BLS OEWS</a>).</p>
          <p>Metro/nonmetro cuts from the same table:</p>
          <p>| Area | Employment | Location quotient | Mean hourly | |---|---:|---:|---:| | Little Rock–NLR–Conway | 4,590 | 2.01 | $16.19 | | Hot Springs | 610 | 2.42 | $14.81 | | Jonesboro | 690 | 1.78 | $15.44 | | North Arkansas nonmetro | 1,380 | 1.77 | $14.90 |</p>
          <p>Source: <a class="text-link" href="https://www.bls.gov/oes/2023/may/oes434171.htm" rel="noopener">BLS OEWS May 2023</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">How many small businesses already use AI?</h2>
        <div class="article-body">
          <p>Nationally, AI use is still early outside the loud demos.</p>
          <p>SBA Advocacy, citing Census <strong>BTOS</strong>, reports that <strong>7.6%</strong> of businesses used AI between September 2024 and August 2025. Firms with <strong>250+</strong> employees: <strong>11.4%</strong>. Firms with <strong>fewer than five</strong> employees: <strong>8.2%</strong> (<a class="text-link" href="https://advocacy.sba.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/FINAL_FAQsAboutSmallBusiness_2026_012826.pdf" rel="noopener">SBA FAQ, Feb 2026</a>).</p>
          <p>Home service owners report higher operational AI use in Jobber’s 2026 survey: <strong>52%</strong> use AI day-to-day. <strong>88%</strong> of high-confidence businesses use AI versus <strong>27%</strong> of low-confidence peers. <strong>64%</strong> of owners under 30 already use AI. Common tasks: quoting (<strong>54%</strong>), invoicing (<strong>52%</strong>), business writing (<strong>51%</strong>) (<a class="text-link" href="https://www.getjobber.com/home-service-trends-report/" rel="noopener">Jobber 2026</a>).</p>
          <p>Those two datasets measure different things (economy-wide BTOS vs home-service owner survey). Cite both with the population named.</p>
        </div>
        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">What does this mean for AI receptionists?</h2>
        <div class="article-body">
          <p>Put the pieces together without inventing a market-size fairy tale:</p>
          <ul><li>Local service demand still arrives by <strong>phone</strong> (ServiceTitan <strong>64%</strong> dominant channel).</li><li>A large share of those calls never reach a person (Invoca <strong>52%</strong> speak-to-person; CallRail industry miss rates; 411 Locals’ older <strong>62%</strong> field result).</li><li>Unanswered callers often <strong>do not retry</strong> (CallRail <strong>up to 85%</strong>).</li><li>Customers expect <strong>fast</strong> replies (Jobber: <strong>55%+</strong> within an hour).</li><li>Human receptionist capacity is expensive relative to SMB margins, and BLS expects the occupation to <strong>shrink</strong> as automation spreads — while Arkansas still runs a <strong>above-average</strong> receptionist density.</li></ul>
          <p>An AI receptionist does not replace judgment-heavy work. It covers overflow, after-hours, and the “nobody can pick up while we’re on the job” gap — the exact failure mode of nonemployer and small-crew firms that dominate Arkansas’s <strong>292,728</strong> small businesses.</p>
        </div>
        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Methodology</h2>
        <div class="article-body">
          <ul><li>We prefer <strong>primary publishers</strong>: BLS, Census (via SBA Advocacy), CallRail’s own report PDF, Invoca’s own benchmarks page, ServiceTitan’s press summary of its contractor survey, Jobber’s own trends report, and dated field research (411 Locals).</li><li>We <strong>do not</strong> treat competitor AI-receptionist marketing blogs as primary sources for Key Takeaways.</li><li>When a widely circulated number comes from an old study (411 Locals <strong>2016</strong>), we keep the original date visible.</li><li>Full machine-readable table: aa-missed-call-stats-verified.csv (110 verified rows as of 2026-09-12).</li><li>This page will be refreshed when BLS publishes newer state OEWS files or SBA updates Arkansas/US profiles.</li></ul>
        </div>
        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">About Arkansas Automated</h2>
        <div class="article-body">
          <p>Arkansas Automated builds AI operating systems for Arkansas SMBs — front desk, follow-up, and booking — with a Fort Smith / NWA focus. If you need the phone answered when your crew is on a roof or in an attic, <a class="text-link" href="https://arkansasautomated.com/contact" rel="noopener">talk to us</a>.</p>
                  </div>
      </div>
    </section>
